@lang en

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `uk-interpretation-time`

-- Tests for uk-interpretation-time.l4.
--
-- Every expected value below was decided by reading the section it cites, and written down before the module was run.
-- A failing assertion is a finding: no expected value is edited to match what the code computed.
-- A `#ASSERT REFUSED ... BECAUSE` pins both that the module refuses and the exact reason it gives; the reason names what
-- the Interpretation Act 1978 does not say. The Act has no rule for the six period cases below, so each is refused.

§ `Fixtures`

GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of enactment`
      d    IS A DATE
      provision IS A `Provision on coming into force`
GIVETH AN `An enactment`
`an enactment` kind d provision MEANS `An enactment` WITH
    `its kind`                             IS kind
    `the day it was passed or made`        IS d
    `what it says about coming into force` IS provision

GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A DATE
`the day on which it comes into force` e MEANS (`section 4 — when the enactment comes into force` e)'s `the day`

GIVEN e IS AN `An enactment`
GIVETH A `Moment within a day`
`the moment on that day at which it comes into force` e MEANS (`section 4 — when the enactment comes into force` e)'s `the moment within the day`

-- Days of rest, recess and holiday are an input. Saturday 14 and Sunday 15 March 2026 are a weekend; Monday 16 March 2026
-- is supplied as a holiday, so the three days in a row are all days of rest or holiday.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a weekend day or the supplied holiday` d MEANS
        `is weekend` d
    OR  d EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 16)

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`no day is a day of rest` d MEANS FALSE

§ `Section 4: time of commencement`

§§ `The three commencement cases of the brief`

-- Case 1. An Act is passed (Royal Assent) on 2026-03-10 and says nothing about when it comes into force.
-- s 4(b): "where no provision is made for its coming into force, at the beginning of the day on which the Act receives the
-- Royal Assent". The day is 2026-03-10 and the moment is the beginning of that day.
-- The text's trigger is the Royal Assent, not publication: "published" is not a trigger anywhere in the text (fork F10).
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 10)
#ASSERT `the moment on that day at which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) EQUALS `the beginning of the day`
#ASSERT `section 4(b) applies to` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`)

-- Case 2. An Act says it comes into force on a day to be fixed later, and the day is fixed as 2026-06-01.
-- s 4(a): provision is made for it to come into force on a particular day, so "at the beginning of that day" (fork F5).
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a day to be fixed later` (JUST (YMD 2026 6 1)))) EQUALS (YMD 2026 6 1)
#ASSERT `the moment on that day at which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a day to be fixed later` (JUST (YMD 2026 6 1)))) EQUALS `the beginning of the day`
-- Before the day is fixed, neither limb gives a day.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a day to be fixed later` NOTHING)) BECAUSE "the enactment provides for a day to be fixed later and none is fixed yet, so neither limb of section 4 gives a day"

-- Case 3. Subordinate legislation is made on 2026-03-10 and says nothing about when it comes into force.
-- s 23(1) excepts s 4(b) from the provisions that apply to subordinate legislation, and Sch 2 para 6 lists only s 4(a)
-- for subordinate legislation made before 1979: the Act gives no day.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on when subordinate legislation that makes no provision comes into force"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the moment on that day at which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on when subordinate legislation that makes no provision comes into force"
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(b) applies to` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`)

§§ `Every branch of section 4`

-- (a) a particular day: an Act, "at the beginning of that day".
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 4 1))) EQUALS (YMD 2026 4 1)
#ASSERT `the moment on that day at which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 4 1))) EQUALS `the beginning of the day`
-- (a) a particular day that is the very day of the Royal Assent: still the beginning of that day.
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 3 10))) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 10)
-- (a) a particular day earlier than the passing: s 4 does not forbid it, and gives the day named.
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 3 1))) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 1)
-- (a) subordinate legislation that names a day: s 4(a) applies to it (s 23(1)).
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 4 1))) EQUALS (YMD 2026 4 1)
#ASSERT `the moment on that day at which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 4 1))) EQUALS `the beginning of the day`
#ASSERT `section 4(a) applies to` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 4 1)))
-- (a) subordinate legislation whose day is fixed later, once fixed.
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a day to be fixed later` (JUST (YMD 2026 7 1)))) EQUALS (YMD 2026 7 1)
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a day to be fixed later` NOTHING)) BECAUSE "the enactment provides for a day to be fixed later and none is fixed yet, so neither limb of section 4 gives a day"
-- The Schedule 1 definition of "commencement" is the same answer.
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the commencement of the enactment` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`))'s `the day` EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 10)
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the commencement of the enactment` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) (`a particular day` (YMD 2026 4 1))))'s `the day` EQUALS (YMD 2026 4 1)

§§ `Section 26: the Act's own commencement, and the years the section reaches`

-- s 26: "This Act shall come into force on 1st January 1979." The Act was passed on 20 July 1978 (the date printed
-- under its long title in the text). s 22(1): "This Act applies to itself".
#ASSERT `the day on which the Interpretation Act 1978 comes into force` EQUALS (YMD 1979 1 1)
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1978 7 20) (`a particular day` (YMD 1979 1 1))) EQUALS (YMD 1979 1 1)
#ASSERT `the moment on that day at which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1978 7 20) (`a particular day` (YMD 1979 1 1))) EQUALS `the beginning of the day`
#ASSERT NOT `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (YMD 1978 12 31)
#ASSERT `the day is on or after the commencement of the Interpretation Act 1978` (YMD 1979 1 1)

-- Sch 2 para 3: s 4 applies to Acts passed after the year 1889. 31 December 1889 is not after the year 1889; 1 January 1890 is.
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(a) applies to` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1889 12 31) (`a particular day` (YMD 1890 1 1)))
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(b) applies to` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1889 12 31) `no provision`)
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1889 12 31) (`a particular day` (YMD 1890 1 1))) BECAUSE "section 4 does not apply to an enactment passed or made in or before 1889, and the Interpretation Act 1978 has no other rule on its coming into force"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1889 12 31) `no provision`) BECAUSE "section 4 does not apply to an enactment passed or made in or before 1889, and the Interpretation Act 1978 has no other rule on its coming into force"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1889 12 31) (`a day to be fixed later` (JUST (YMD 1890 6 1)))) BECAUSE "section 4 does not apply to an enactment passed or made in or before 1889, and the Interpretation Act 1978 has no other rule on its coming into force"
#ASSERT `section 4(a) applies to` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1890 1 1) (`a particular day` (YMD 1890 6 1)))
#ASSERT `section 4(b) applies to` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1890 1 1) `no provision`)
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1890 1 1) `no provision`) EQUALS (YMD 1890 1 1)
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1890 1 1) (`a particular day` (YMD 1890 6 1))) EQUALS (YMD 1890 6 1)

-- Sch 2 para 6: s 4(a) applies to subordinate legislation made before the commencement "as they apply to Acts passed at that time".
-- Made on 31 December 1978, after 1889: s 4(a) applies. Made on 31 December 1889: it does not. Made on 1 January 1890: it does.
#ASSERT `section 4(a) applies to` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1978 12 31) (`a particular day` (YMD 1979 2 1)))
#ASSERT `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1978 12 31) (`a particular day` (YMD 1979 2 1))) EQUALS (YMD 1979 2 1)
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(a) applies to` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1889 12 31) (`a particular day` (YMD 1890 2 1)))
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1889 12 31) (`a particular day` (YMD 1890 2 1))) BECAUSE "section 4 does not apply to an enactment passed or made in or before 1889, and the Interpretation Act 1978 has no other rule on its coming into force"
#ASSERT `section 4(a) applies to` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1890 1 1) (`a particular day` (YMD 1890 2 1)))
-- Subordinate legislation made before 1979 that says nothing: s 4(b) is not among the provisions Sch 2 para 6 applies.
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(b) applies to` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1950 5 5) `no provision`)
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1950 5 5) `no provision`) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on when subordinate legislation that makes no provision comes into force"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day on which it comes into force` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1979 1 1) `no provision`) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on when subordinate legislation that makes no provision comes into force"

§ `Section 9: references to time of day`

#ASSERT `section 9 — the time standard of an expression of time in an Act` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `Greenwich mean time`
-- Subject to the Summer Time Act 1972 s 3, whose text is not among the sources.
#ASSERT REFUSED `section 9 — the time standard of an expression of time in an Act` FALSE TRUE BECAUSE "section 9 is subject to section 3 of the Summer Time Act 1972, which is not among the sources, and the time falls in a period of summer time"
-- "unless it is otherwise specifically stated".
#ASSERT REFUSED `section 9 — the time standard of an expression of time in an Act` TRUE FALSE BECAUSE "the time is otherwise specifically stated, so section 9 does not say what standard it is in"
-- Both: the summer-time limb is tried first, as the section opens "Subject to".
#ASSERT REFUSED `section 9 — the time standard of an expression of time in an Act` TRUE TRUE BECAUSE "section 9 is subject to section 3 of the Summer Time Act 1972, which is not among the sources, and the time falls in a period of summer time"
-- Reading A of fork F6: the beginning of the day, as a clock reading, is 00:00:00 Greenwich mean time.
#ASSERT (`section 4 read with section 9 — the clock reading of the moment` `the beginning of the day` FALSE)'s `hour` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`section 4 read with section 9 — the clock reading of the moment` `the beginning of the day` FALSE)'s `minute` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`section 4 read with section 9 — the clock reading of the moment` `the beginning of the day` FALSE)'s `second` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`section 4 read with section 9 — the clock reading of the moment` `the beginning of the day` FALSE)'s `standard` EQUALS `Greenwich mean time`
#ASSERT REFUSED `section 4 read with section 9 — the clock reading of the moment` `the beginning of the day` TRUE BECAUSE "section 9 is subject to section 3 of the Summer Time Act 1972, which is not among the sources, and the time falls in a period of summer time"

§ `Section 13: anticipatory exercise of powers`

`an anticipatory power that meets the conditions` MEANS `An anticipatory power` WITH
    `the day the Act was passed` IS YMD 2026 3 10
    `the Act or provision does not come into force immediately on its passing` IS TRUE
    `the power is to make subordinate legislation, make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act` IS TRUE
    `it is necessary or expedient for bringing the Act or a provision into force, or for giving full effect to it at or after the time it comes into force` IS TRUE
    `the contrary intention appears` IS FALSE

`an anticipatory power for an Act that comes into force at once` MEANS `An anticipatory power` WITH
    `the day the Act was passed` IS YMD 2026 3 10
    `the Act or provision does not come into force immediately on its passing` IS FALSE
    `the power is to make subordinate legislation, make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act` IS TRUE
    `it is necessary or expedient for bringing the Act or a provision into force, or for giving full effect to it at or after the time it comes into force` IS TRUE
    `the contrary intention appears` IS FALSE

`an anticipatory power for a purpose outside the section` MEANS `An anticipatory power` WITH
    `the day the Act was passed` IS YMD 2026 3 10
    `the Act or provision does not come into force immediately on its passing` IS TRUE
    `the power is to make subordinate legislation, make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act` IS TRUE
    `it is necessary or expedient for bringing the Act or a provision into force, or for giving full effect to it at or after the time it comes into force` IS FALSE
    `the contrary intention appears` IS FALSE

`an anticipatory power that is not for the purposes of the Act` MEANS `An anticipatory power` WITH
    `the day the Act was passed` IS YMD 2026 3 10
    `the Act or provision does not come into force immediately on its passing` IS TRUE
    `the power is to make subordinate legislation, make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act` IS FALSE
    `it is necessary or expedient for bringing the Act or a provision into force, or for giving full effect to it at or after the time it comes into force` IS TRUE
    `the contrary intention appears` IS FALSE

`an anticipatory power displaced by a contrary intention` MEANS `An anticipatory power` WITH
    `the day the Act was passed` IS YMD 2026 3 10
    `the Act or provision does not come into force immediately on its passing` IS TRUE
    `the power is to make subordinate legislation, make appointments, give notices, prescribe forms or do any other thing for the purposes of the Act` IS TRUE
    `it is necessary or expedient for bringing the Act or a provision into force, or for giving full effect to it at or after the time it comes into force` IS TRUE
    `the contrary intention appears` IS TRUE

#ASSERT `the conditions of section 13 are met by` `an anticipatory power that meets the conditions`
#ASSERT NOT `the conditions of section 13 are met by` `an anticipatory power for an Act that comes into force at once`
#ASSERT NOT `the conditions of section 13 are met by` `an anticipatory power for a purpose outside the section`
#ASSERT NOT `the conditions of section 13 are met by` `an anticipatory power that is not for the purposes of the Act`
#ASSERT NOT `the conditions of section 13 are met by` `an anticipatory power displaced by a contrary intention`

-- An instrument that comes into force at the beginning of a later day is after the passing.
#ASSERT `section 13 permits an instrument made under the Act to come into force at` `an anticipatory power that meets the conditions` (`Coming into force` WITH `the day` IS YMD 2026 3 11, `the moment within the day` IS `the beginning of the day`)
-- At the beginning of the day of the passing is before the passing (fork F8): s 13 does not give the permission.
#ASSERT NOT `section 13 permits an instrument made under the Act to come into force at` `an anticipatory power that meets the conditions` (`Coming into force` WITH `the day` IS YMD 2026 3 10, `the moment within the day` IS `the beginning of the day`)
#ASSERT NOT `section 13 permits an instrument made under the Act to come into force at` `an anticipatory power that meets the conditions` (`Coming into force` WITH `the day` IS YMD 2026 3 9, `the moment within the day` IS `the beginning of the day`)
#ASSERT NOT `section 13 permits an instrument made under the Act to come into force at` `an anticipatory power displaced by a contrary intention` (`Coming into force` WITH `the day` IS YMD 2026 3 11, `the moment within the day` IS `the beginning of the day`)
-- The power may be exercised on a later day; on the same day the input has no moment, so the module refuses.
#ASSERT `section 13 permits the power to be exercised on` `an anticipatory power that meets the conditions` (YMD 2026 3 11)
#ASSERT REFUSED `section 13 permits the power to be exercised on` `an anticipatory power that meets the conditions` (YMD 2026 3 10) BECAUSE "section 13 says after the passing, and a day alone does not say whether a moment on the day of passing is after it"
#ASSERT NOT `section 13 permits the power to be exercised on` `an anticipatory power that meets the conditions` (YMD 2026 3 9)
#ASSERT NOT `section 13 permits the power to be exercised on` `an anticipatory power for an Act that comes into force at once` (YMD 2026 3 11)
-- The conditions fail, so the answer is FALSE before the same-day question is reached.
#ASSERT NOT `section 13 permits the power to be exercised on` `an anticipatory power for an Act that comes into force at once` (YMD 2026 3 10)

§ `Schedule 1: month, financial year, commencement`

-- "Month" means calendar month. [1850]  Sch 2 para 4(1)(a): a year in brackets bars Acts passed in or before that year.
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1850 12 31) `no provision`)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1851 1 1) `no provision`)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1978 12 31) `no provision`)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the meaning of month in` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) EQUALS `a calendar month`
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the meaning of month in` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1850 12 31) `no provision`) BECAUSE "Schedule 1 does not define month for this enactment"
-- Subordinate legislation made after the commencement takes Schedule 1 as Acts do (s 23(1)); made before it, only the
-- expressions Sch 2 para 6 lists, and "month" is not among them.
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1979 1 1) `no provision`)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`)
#ASSERT NOT `Schedule 1 defines month as a calendar month for` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1978 12 31) `no provision`)
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the meaning of month in` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1978 12 31) `no provision`) BECAUSE "Schedule 1 does not define month for this enactment"

-- "Financial year" means ... the twelve months ending with 31st March. [1889]
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2027)'s `first day` EQUALS (YMD 2026 4 1)
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2027)'s `last day` EQUALS (YMD 2027 3 31)
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2024)'s `first day` EQUALS (YMD 2023 4 1)
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2024)'s `last day` EQUALS (YMD 2024 3 31)
-- 29 February 2024 is in the financial year ending 31 March 2024; 31 March 2024 is its last day; 1 April 2024 is outside it.
#ASSERT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2024 2 29) (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2024)
#ASSERT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2024 3 31) (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2024)
#ASSERT NOT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2024 4 1) (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2024)
#ASSERT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2023 4 1) (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2024)
#ASSERT NOT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2023 3 31) (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) TRUE 2024)
-- The definition is limited to certain matters.
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 2026 3 10) `no provision`) FALSE 2027 BECAUSE "Schedule 1 defines financial year only in relation to the Consolidated Fund, the National Loans Fund, moneys provided by Parliament, the Exchequer, or central taxes or finance"
-- [1889]: Acts passed after the year 1889; and, for subordinate legislation, those made after the commencement.
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1889 12 31) `no provision`) TRUE 1890 BECAUSE "Schedule 1 does not define financial year for this enactment"
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `an Act` (YMD 1890 1 1) `no provision`) TRUE 1891)'s `first day` EQUALS (YMD 1890 4 1)
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1978 12 31) `no provision`) TRUE 1979 BECAUSE "Schedule 1 does not define financial year for this enactment"
#ASSERT (`Schedule 1 — the financial year` (`an enactment` `subordinate legislation` (YMD 1979 1 1) `no provision`) TRUE 1980)'s `last day` EQUALS (YMD 1980 3 31)

§ `Periods of days, weeks, months and years: the six worked cases of the brief, and the branches around them`

-- The Interpretation Act 1978 has no section that counts a period, and it defines neither "day", "week" nor "year".
-- Each case below is therefore refused, with the reason that names what is missing. No date is computed.
-- Not imported, on purpose: daydate's `add months` clamp (see tests-daydate-not-used.l4), the "beginning with" idiom
-- of the phrasebook, and any rule of the common law or of another jurisdiction.

-- three years from 2025-07-16
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` 3 (YMD 2025 7 16) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"
-- five years from 2025-01-15, and from 2025-09-01
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` 5 (YMD 2025 1 15) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` 5 (YMD 2025 9 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"
-- thirty days from 2026-01-01
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of days counted from a day` 30 (YMD 2026 1 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
-- two years from 2024-02-28
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` 2 (YMD 2024 2 28) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"
-- one year from 2024-02-29 (a start on a leap day)
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2024 2 29) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"
-- one month from 2025-01-31 (a start on the 31st, and a month that lacks the start day's number)
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2025 1 31) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"

-- Periods of one day, one week, one month, one year.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of days counted from a day` 1 (YMD 2026 1 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of weeks counted from a day` 1 (YMD 2026 1 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of weeks from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2025 1 15) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2025 1 15) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"
-- Starts on the last day of a month, on a leap day, and the month that lacks the day.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2024 1 31) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2024 2 29) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2025 2 28) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` 12 (YMD 2024 2 29) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of months counted after an event` 6 (YMD 2025 8 31) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of years counted after an event` 4 (YMD 2024 2 29) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define year and has no rule for counting a period of years after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of days counted from a day` 7 (YMD 2026 12 31) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of weeks counted from a day` 2 (YMD 2024 2 29) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of weeks from a day"
-- Zero is not special either: the Act says nothing.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period of days counted from a day` 0 (YMD 2026 1 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"

-- Whether a day falls inside a period counted from a day: it needs the last day, which no section gives. The day before
-- the start, the start, the day that would be last on each reading, and the day after are all refused alike, so that no
-- reading of "from" (counting the start day or not) is smuggled in.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day falls inside a period of days counted from a day` 30 (YMD 2026 1 1) (YMD 2025 12 31) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day falls inside a period of days counted from a day` 30 (YMD 2026 1 1) (YMD 2026 1 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day falls inside a period of days counted from a day` 30 (YMD 2026 1 1) (YMD 2026 1 30) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day falls inside a period of days counted from a day` 30 (YMD 2026 1 1) (YMD 2026 1 31) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day falls inside a period of days counted from a day` 30 (YMD 2026 1 1) (YMD 2026 2 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule for counting a period of days from a day"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day falls inside a period of months counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2025 1 31) (YMD 2025 2 28) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the day falls inside a period of months counted after an event` 1 (YMD 2025 1 31) (YMD 2025 3 1) BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 defines a month as a calendar month but has no rule for counting a period of months after an event"

§ `Days of rest, recess and holiday: an input, and no rule of the Act`

-- The Act has no rule that moves the last day of a period, so every case below is refused, whether or not the last day
-- is a day of rest, and whether one day or several in a row are.
-- Saturday 14 March 2026 and Sunday 15 March 2026 are a weekend; Monday 16 March 2026 is supplied as a holiday.
#ASSERT `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` (YMD 2026 3 14)
#ASSERT `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` (YMD 2026 3 15)
#ASSERT `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` (YMD 2026 3 16)
#ASSERT NOT `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` (YMD 2026 3 13)
#ASSERT NOT `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` (YMD 2026 3 17)
-- 11 July 2026 is a Saturday: the later last day used below for a period counted in months or years.
#ASSERT `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` (YMD 2026 7 11)
-- A last day that is a weekday and not a holiday.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period once days of rest, recess and holidays are considered` (YMD 2026 3 13) `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on a period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday"
-- A last day that is a Saturday: one day of rest, followed by two more.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period once days of rest, recess and holidays are considered` (YMD 2026 3 14) `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on a period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday"
-- A last day that is a Sunday: two days of rest in a row, followed by one more.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period once days of rest, recess and holidays are considered` (YMD 2026 3 15) `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on a period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday"
-- A last day that is the Monday holiday: the last of three in a row.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period once days of rest, recess and holidays are considered` (YMD 2026 3 16) `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on a period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday"
-- A calendar with no day of rest at all.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period once days of rest, recess and holidays are considered` (YMD 2026 3 14) `no day is a day of rest` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on a period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday"
-- A period of months or years whose last day is a day of rest: the same silence, so months and years are not treated apart
-- from days (fork F3): the Act has no rule for any of them.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the last day of a period once days of rest, recess and holidays are considered` (YMD 2026 7 11) `a weekend day or the supplied holiday` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 has no rule on a period whose last day is a day of rest, recess or holiday"

§ `The closed interval of a period (not a rule of the Act)`

-- A helper only: the caller supplies the first and last day and both are in the period.
`the period from 2026-03-10 to 2026-03-12` MEANS `A period of days` WITH
    `first day` IS YMD 2026 3 10
    `last day`  IS YMD 2026 3 12

`the period of the one day 2026-03-10` MEANS `A period of days` WITH
    `first day` IS YMD 2026 3 10
    `last day`  IS YMD 2026 3 10

#ASSERT NOT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 9) `the period from 2026-03-10 to 2026-03-12`
#ASSERT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 10) `the period from 2026-03-10 to 2026-03-12`
#ASSERT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 11) `the period from 2026-03-10 to 2026-03-12`
#ASSERT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 12) `the period from 2026-03-10 to 2026-03-12`
#ASSERT NOT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 13) `the period from 2026-03-10 to 2026-03-12`
#ASSERT NOT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 9) `the period of the one day 2026-03-10`
#ASSERT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 10) `the period of the one day 2026-03-10`
#ASSERT NOT `the day falls inside the period` (YMD 2026 3 11) `the period of the one day 2026-03-10`

§ `Schedule 1: the time words, in the text's own words`

-- The text defines "month" and "financial year" and has no entry for "day", "week" or "year".
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the meaning of the time word, in the text's own words` `the word month` EQUALS "calendar month"
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the meaning of the time word, in the text's own words` `the phrase financial year` EQUALS "the twelve months ending with 31st March"
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the meaning of the time word, in the text's own words` `the word day` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year"
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the meaning of the time word, in the text's own words` `the word week` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year"
#ASSERT REFUSED `Schedule 1 — the meaning of the time word, in the text's own words` `the word year` BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act 1978 does not define day, week or year"
